# 074.DNADuplex -- the bonded model on B-DNA

A 2 x 21 nucleotide B-DNA duplex under the full nucleic model: AMBER bonds,
valence angles and torsions on top of the articulated rigid-body mesh, with
real charges, radii and masses.

    make prep     build dna.nc
    make point    the energy table below
    make run      dynamics


## What it is for

The protein examples (070-073) exercise phi/psi/omega/chi. This one exercises
what a nucleotide has instead: six backbone torsions per residue rather than
three (alpha, beta, gamma, delta, epsilon, zeta), the glycosidic chi, and a
five-membered sugar that no protein has an equivalent of.

Point energy at the input conformation:

    stretch     1020.80 kJ/mol
    bend        2403.61 kJ/mol
    dihedral    3046.03 kJ/mol

against AMBER's own value for the same axes, 3214.5 kJ/mol of torsion: -5.2%
over 448 axes. Per-axis torque agreement runs 0.86 to 0.98 (correlation)
with 0 to 3 sign flips per axis type.


## The furanose is RIGID, and that is the model's one real compromise

No STRETCH, no BEND and no torsion is emitted inside the five ring atoms
(C1'-C2'-C3'-C4'-O4'), so what holds them is the .rbody mesh alone, at
whatever --stiffness the build used. delta is frozen with the ring, since it
turns about C4'-C3'.

Why: the ghost-ring construction reproduces a torsion as springs between
virtual sites placed by rotating REAL atoms, so its energy is not a function
of the dihedral alone -- deforming the ring's valence angles moves the ghosts
too, and there is a relaxation channel where every ghost-ghost distance
reaches its equilibrium and the torsion energy collapses. One deformation
relaxes all five ring axes at once, so the ring flattens. Measured before
freezing: the pucker amplitude fell from 37 to 13 degrees on all 42 sugars,
where AMBER's own bonded terms hold it at 41.

The consequence to know: a frozen ring is only as rigid as --stiffness. At
250 kJ/mol/A^2, the value the protein examples use, the ring is ten times
softer than the real bonds around it and relaxes anyway. Hence 3000 here,
and 0.3 fs in model.msp -- the two are tied, a spring N times stiffer needing
a timestep divided by sqrt(N).

After 6 ps at that setting, over the 42 sugars:

    amplitude   37.0 -> 30.9 degrees
    C2'-endo (South, the B-form state)   36% -> 57%
    C3'-endo (North)                      0% ->  5%
    intermediate                         64% -> 38%

The B-form majority is kept and even sharpens. This is a quench, though --
viscosity with no random force -- so it says a state is preserved, not that
its population is predicted.


## What this cannot show

No IMPALA and no hydrophobicity: neither has a published nucleic
parameterisation, so amber.dna.nbi.ff carries 0 in both columns rather than
an invented number.

Terminal residues are approximate to about 0.27 e. A PDB spells a 5' or 3'
residue with the plain name (DA, not DA5), so a .grp cannot tell them apart
and they take the interior charges.

The pucker is preserved, not predicted: freeing the ring would need a
construction whose energy depends on the dihedral alone.
